<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raichur Doab</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> Doab is the triangle of fertile land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Tungabhadra rivers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, named after the fortified town of Raichur. From the 14th to the 16th century it was the chief prize in recurring wars between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and its successor sultanates, and its forts of Raichur and Mudgal changed hands many times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raichur-doab.jpg" alt="Raichur Doab" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jungpionier · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karnataka_topo_deu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
